<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</h1><p class="st-meta">1808 BCE – 1776 BCE (approx.) · capital Shubat-Enlil</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The short-lived realm of the Amorite king Shamshi-Adad I, who united northern Mesopotamia from the Zagros to the Euphrates and ruled through his sons.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a>, an Amorite chief, seized Ekallatum on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and then the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a> around 1808 BCE. He went on to take <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> plains, creating the first state to unify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/apum">Shubat-Enlil</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-leilan">Tell Leilan</a>).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a> ruled as 'king of the universe', placing his elder son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishme-dagan-i">Ishme-Dagan</a> at Ekallatum and his younger son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasmah-addu">Yasmah-Addu</a> at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> archives preserve his letters, in which he scolds Yasmah-Addu for idleness and directs campaigns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> and harvests. The kingdom was a network of fortified cities, tribal allies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, held together by the king's personal energy. Later Assyrian tradition counted him among the kings of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a> died around 1776 BCE, the kingdom fell apart. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> recovered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a>, local kings rose at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-andarig">Andarig</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-karana">Karana</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kurda">Kurda</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishme-dagan-i">Ishme-Dagan</a> kept only the core around Ekallatum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia" width="320" height="252"><figcaption>Image: Near_East_topographic_map-blank.svg: Sémhur derivative work: Zunkir (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samsi_Addu.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1808 BCE – 1776 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shubat-Enlil</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shamshi-Adad I, Ishme-Dagan I, Yasmah-Addu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesopotamian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akkadian, Amorite</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>, <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shamshi-Adad's kingdom, Ekallatum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1792&amp;focus=kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia" aria-label="Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5656 919 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6039 1183h0M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5976 1175h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5976 1175h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Syria. <a href="/atlas?year=-1792&amp;focus=kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
